  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to see how the panels render, and I haven't checked that the rule runs cleanly or what it matches.\n\n**Data:** the logs index has `timestamp`, `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `request`, `url` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-59285691 Web logs overview\" has four panels, at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-59285691-web-logs-overview-8b9a321178a8\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Requests over time (area chart)\n- Requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar chart)\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-59285691 5xx spike\" (id `cc6541f6-91df-4f85-a426-7d1917cd0b02`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in `kibana_sample_data_logs` whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10. It has no action attached, so it only records alerts in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.\n\nThe rule was created disabled, so I enabled it as a second step.",
